  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The dot product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the dot-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(u\\,i-2\\,j-2\\,k)\\cdot (4\\,i-2\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =4u+4-2 \\\\  & =4u+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,$ we must have $4u+2=0,$ which means that $u=-\\frac{1}{2}.$",
